# Engineering Standards

## Specification-Driven Development

- Follow the approved specification and technical plan.
- Do not implement functionality outside the approved scope.
- Clarify ambiguous requirements before implementation.
- Keep implementation tasks small and independently verifiable.

## Test-Driven Development

Use strict Red-Green-Refactor:

1. Write a failing test.
2. Run the test and verify it fails for the expected reason.
3. Implement the minimum production code required.
4. Run the test suite.
5. Refactor without changing observable behaviour.
6. Run the complete test suite again.

Never generate an entire feature and its tests in one step.

## Architecture

- Use C# and .NET 10.
- Follow SOLID principles.
- Prefer simple domain modelling.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions.
- Do not introduce APIs, databases or user interfaces.
- Use dependency injection only where it adds value.

## Code Quality

- Enable nullable reference types.
- Enable implicit usings.
- Treat compiler warnings as errors.
- Use meaningful names.
- Keep methods focused.
- Prefer deterministic behaviour.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

## Testing

- Use xUnit.
- Cover normal behaviour, edge cases and invalid inputs.
- Verify behaviour rather than implementation details.
- Keep tests independent.
- Do not modify tests merely to make an incorrect implementation pass.

## Git

- Never commit automatically.
- The developer controls all commits.
- Never amend or squash commits without approval.
- Never add Co-authored-by trailers.
- Never attribute commits to Copilot, Cursor or Claude.
- Never rewrite Git history without explicit approval.

## AI Collaboration

- Explain proposed changes before implementation.
- Work on one task at a time.
- Identify assumptions.
- Explain architectural trade-offs.
- Request human review at each TDD checkpoint.
